@ 7.12 - Proto-Algonquian to Menominee
# citation: Whimemsz, from Hockett, C. F. (1981), “The Phonological History of Menominee”.
# 			Anthropological Linguistics 23(2): 51-87; and Miner, Kenneth L. (1979), “Theoretical
# 			Implications of the Great Menominee Vowel Shift”. Kansas Working Papers in Linguistics
# 			4(1): 7-25.
	we je > o i / _C
	we > o / #_
	θ > s / _O
	θ > r
	V:[- long] > ∅ / _# “[does not apply in disyllabic words containing two short vowels]”
	∅ > h / V:[- long]_#
	H > ∅ / _m
	{s,r} > h / _O
	w > ∅ / h_V
	a > o / $am_w
	V > V:[+ long] “when V is the second vowel of a word and follows a short-vowel
				syllable. Does not apply in glottal words”
	e > i / V:[+ long]%_ // _H
	N > h / _{O,r}
	e > i / #(C)_ // _H
	e > i / _{k,m} “when in the second syllable of glottal words”
	{w,j} > ∅ / C_#
	C > ∅ / C_#
	w{ i, i:[+ long] } > { o, o:[+ long] } / C_w
	ʃ tʃ > s ts
	V:[+ long] > V:[- long] / CC(G)_C{V,#} “[i.e., when following a cluster but not followed by a cluster. Only
				applies ‘after the first long vowel of a nonglottal word, and everywhere in a
				glottal word’]”
	V > V:[+ long] / _CC in even syllables
	V:[+ long] > V:[- long] / _C{V,#} in even syllables; “does not apply in the second syllable of a non-glottal
				word”
	{ e, e:[+ long] } i > æ(ː) e
	i:[+ long] o:[+ long] oʔ > e:[+ long] u:[+ long] uʔ “[blocked when i:[+ long] or a C+G sequence follows
				anywhere in the word, but does apply if æ(ː) intervenes before any following i:[+
				long] or C+G]”
	{wi:[+ long],ji:[+ long],we:[+ long],je:[+ long],wæ:[+ long],jæ:[+ long]}
				{wi,ji,we,je,wæ,jæ} > i:[+ long] i / C_
	æ > e / in odd syllables // _{w,j,H}
	r > n
	wa ja > uə̯ iə̯ / C_